@model GlassOnTheStreet.Web.Controllers.HomePageViewModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Glass on the Street — Minneapolis Car Break-In Map";
    var stats = Model.Stats;
    var gap = Model.ReportingGap;
    var changeText = stats.PercentChange is null
        ? "—"
        : $"{(stats.PercentChange > 0 ? "+" : "")}{stats.PercentChange}%";
    var changeClass = stats.PercentChange is > 0 ? "up" : stats.PercentChange is < 0 ? "down" : "";
    ViewData["Description"] =
        "Most car break-ins in Minneapolis never get reported, and the city's own data can't tell a smashed window from an unlocked car. " +
        "Report a break-in in under a minute and help build the dataset residents need to hold the city accountable. " +
        $"{stats.Count} reports in the last 30 days" +
        (stats.PercentChange is null ? "." : $", {changeText} versus the prior 30 days.");
}

<div class="page">
    <p class="kicker">A resident-built accountability tool — Minneapolis</p>
    <h1 class="display" style="font-size: 2.6rem; max-width: 720px;">
        Nobody has real numbers on Minneapolis car break-ins. That's the problem.
    </h1>
    <p class="lede">
        Most break-ins never get reported to police. The ones that do go into
        a system that doesn't even distinguish a smashed window from a car
        someone just tried a door on. Without real numbers, there's nothing
        to put in front of city council, no way to prove where it's getting
        worse, and no one held accountable for fixing it. This is residents
        building that dataset ourselves.
    </p>

    <div class="stat-line">
        <div class="stat">
            <div class="stat-value">@stats.Count</div>
            <div class="stat-label">reports, last 30 days</div>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
            <div class="stat-value @changeClass">@changeText</div>
            <div class="stat-label">vs. the 30 days before that</div>
        </div>
        @if (gap.PercentUnreported is not null)
        {
            <div class="stat">
                <div class="stat-value up">@(gap.PercentUnreported)%</div>
                <div class="stat-label">of reporters never told police</div>
            </div>
        }
    </div>

    <p>
        <a class="btn" asp-controller="Report" asp-action="Index">Report a break-in</a>
        &nbsp;
        <a class="btn btn-outline" asp-controller="Map" asp-action="Index">View the map</a>
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem; font-size: 1.4rem;">Two gaps, one dataset</h2>
    <p>
        <strong>Most break-ins go unreported.</strong> People assume MPD can't
        do anything about a smashed window, so they never call. That means
        official counts are missing an unknown, probably large, share of what's
        actually happening on your block.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Even the reports that exist don't say how it happened.</strong>
        Minneapolis's own open data lumps every car break-in into one category.
        It doesn't record whether a window was smashed or a door was simply
        unlocked — two very different problems with two very different fixes.
        We asked, and the city genuinely doesn't have that field.
    </p>
    <p>
        A resident-reported map with real dates, real locations, and real
        detail on what happened is the only way to close both gaps at once.
        Enough of it, and this becomes something you can hand to a council
        member: here's where it's happening, here's whether it's getting
        better or worse, and here's what the official numbers are missing.
        That's how you hold someone accountable for it.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top: 2.5rem; font-size: 1.4rem;">Your privacy, protected either way</h2>
    <p>
        Locations shown on the map are snapped to the block, never the exact
        address — a pin on someone's house would tell the world who got hit
        and where they park. After you submit a report, we also point you to
        Minneapolis Police Department's online reporting, since this site is
        meant to complement official channels, not replace them.
    </p>
</div>
